\ charset: text in legacy character sets, decoded to Unicode text and (where it is a single-byte set or a
\ Unicode encoding) encoded back. Three kinds of encoding:
\
\   1. Single-byte sets, as FL tables (_tables below): 256 code points per set, in 16 rows of 16 hexadecimal
\      values (row = high nibble of the byte), U+FFFD where a byte is unassigned. Decoding reads the table and
\      encoding inverts it. They are the single-byte encodings of the WHATWG Encoding Standard and the EBCDIC
\      code pages. They are tables rather than the host's TextDecoder because hosts differ: Node 20 decodes
\      windows-1252 as ISO 8859-1 and differs from the standard in ibm866, koi8-u, windows-874, windows-1253
\      and windows-1255, and has no iso-8859-16; the tables give the same text on every host. Adding a set
\      means adding one entry to _tables and its name to the choice lists of decode and encode (the tests
\      check that the lists agree).
\   2. The multi-byte WHATWG encodings (Shift_JIS, GBK, GB18030, Big5, EUC-JP, EUC-KR, ISO-2022-JP), and utf-8,
\      utf-16le and utf-16be: decoded by the private primitive charset:_decode, a thin wrapper over the host
\      TextDecoder (src/prims/charset.ts), because the FL core has no such decoders and the tables are
\      thousands of entries. The host has no encoder for the multi-byte sets (TextEncoder is UTF-8 only), so
\      they are left out of the choice list of charset:encode; utf-8 and utf-16 are encoded here in FL.
\   3. iso-8859-1, true ISO 8859-1 (byte n is U+00n); the WHATWG label of that name means windows-1252.
\
\ Sources of the tables (decoded, byte to Unicode code point):
\   WHATWG single-byte sets: the indexes of the Encoding Standard, https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/index-<name>.txt
\     (the standard is (c) WHATWG, CC BY 4.0, see NOTICE; the indexes list bytes 80 to FF, bytes 00 to 7F are
\     ASCII). They agree with Chromium's TextDecoder for all 256 bytes of every set.
\   EBCDIC 037, 500, 875, 1026: the Unicode Consortium mapping files
\     https://unicode.org/Public/MAPPINGS/VENDORS/MICSFT/EBCDIC/CP037.TXT (and CP500, CP875, CP1026).
\     CP875.TXT assigns U+001A to the six unassigned bytes DC E1 EC ED FC FD, and so do we.
\   EBCDIC 273, 277, 278, 280, 284, 285, 297, 1047, 1140 to 1149: the IBM conversion tables published in the
\     ICU data (icu4c/source/data/mappings/ibm-<n>_P100-199x.ucm), roundtrip (|0) and decode-only (|3)
\     entries. The same ICU tables for 037, 500, 875 and 1026 agree with the Unicode files above, except
\     byte 9A of 1026, which ICU maps to U+20BA and the Unicode file to U+00AA; the Unicode file wins.
\   Cross-checked against Python's cp273/cp500/cp875/cp1026/cp1140 codecs and (037, 500, 1026) the libiconv
\   tables. Each euro page (1140 to 1148) differs from its base page (037, 273, 277, 278, 280, 284, 285, 297,
\   500) at one byte, where the currency sign U+00A4 becomes U+20AC. IBM 1149 is based on 871 (Icelandic),
\   which is not provided; 1149 is included as its own page.
\ Where two bytes map to the same code point (U+001A in 875), encoding picks the lowest byte.
IN: charset

\ ---- the encodings ----

\ Unicode encodings, decoded by the host (strict utf-8 is also what bytes>str does).
: _unicode ( -- list )
  { "utf-8" "utf-16le" "utf-16be" } ;

\ Multi-byte WHATWG encodings, decoded by the host (decode only).
: _multi-byte ( -- list )
  { "gbk" "gb18030" "big5" "euc-jp" "iso-2022-jp" "shift_jis" "euc-kr" } ;

\ The tables: a record of set name to 256 hexadecimal code points.
: _tables ( -- record )
